TITLE:: FxParamUnit
summary:: an effect written as a table of its parameters
categories:: Libraries>FxKit
related:: Classes/FxUnit, Classes/FxDevicePanel, Classes/FxParamPanel, Classes/FxParamView, Classes/FxLoop

DESCRIPTION::
A link::Classes/FxUnit:: whose settings, presets, clipboard, meters and window are worked out from one list. A subclass writes code::*params:: and code::*process::, and usually code::*presetList:: and code::*displayClass::. It runs in a graph, on a bus, or in an link::Classes/FxChain::; if SuperDirtGUI is installed it is an insert too.

Each parameter is also a property, through code::doesNotUnderstand::, so a real method of the same name wins: do not name a parameter code::rate::, code::size::, code::shape:: or code::freeze::. A table with such a name warns when it is first read.

The table's order is what saved node maps carry: append, never reorder.

code::
Ndef(\pad, { Tunnel.ar(Saw.ar(110) ! 2 * 0.2, \tunnel) }).play;
t = Tunnel(Ndef(\pad), \tunnel);
t.feedback = 0.7;
t.get(\feedback);
t.set(\feedback, 0.5);
t.gui;
t.free; Ndef(\pad).clear;
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: params
Subclass: the table, an Array of what code::knob::, code::menu:: and code::toggle:: answer.

METHOD:: process
Subclass: the DSP. sig is an Array of channels, p the parameters by key as control rate signals. Set the Event ctx's code::dry:: when the wet is late, its code::extras:: for what a display wants. Answers the wet.
ARGUMENT:: sig
ARGUMENT:: p
ARGUMENT:: ctx

METHOD:: presetList
Subclass: a name, then the settings that differ from the table's defaults, and so on.

METHOD:: hasTail
Subclass: true if it goes on sounding after its input has stopped.

METHOD:: displayClass
Subclass: its display, a subclass of link::Classes/FxParamView::, or nil.

METHOD:: panelExtras
Subclass: more widgets for the panel's top row.
ARGUMENT:: panel

METHOD:: windowSize
Subclass: a Point.

METHOD:: devicePages
Subclass, for an effect whose window is a link::Classes/FxDevicePanel::: the pages of controls, each a name -> the cards on it, left to right. A card is a section's name (its parameters in table order) or an Event code::(title:, keys:, colour:)::, where a key in code::keys:: may be an Array (a column: two menus stacked) or an Event that is not a parameter (a pad, a menu that acts, a slot that swaps two parameters - see link::Classes/FxDevicePanel::). With code::count: n::, code::keys:: and code::colour:: are functions of the index and the card gets a selector, as Growl's three stages do. The section code::\output:: is never on a page: it is the card at the right of every page. By default one page of every section.

METHOD:: isActive
Subclass: whether a parameter does anything with the others as they are now. The device panel dims what answers false - Growl's crossovers outside multiband, a filter's frequency while it is off. True unless the effect says otherwise.
ARGUMENT:: key
The parameter.
ARGUMENT:: unit
The object, to read the others from.

METHOD:: identityColour
Subclass: the effect family's colour, a link::Classes/Color:: from code::FxPalette.identity:: (code::\reverb::, code::\delay::, code::\spectral::...), or nil for none. The device panel draws the name, the hero knobs' arcs and a card coloured code::\identity:: in it, and the display uses it for what the effect itself adds.

METHOD:: title
What a window calls it.

METHOD:: insertKind
What SuperDirtGUI calls this kind of insert.

METHOD:: insertLabel
What its button says.

METHOD:: keyColour
Subclass: a parameter's own colour in the device panel, over its card's - for a knob that links to something of that colour in the display (Imager's width knobs wear their bands' colours). nil, the card's colour, unless the effect says otherwise.
ARGUMENT:: key
The parameter.

METHOD:: tidalParams
The keys SuperDirtGUI lets a Tidal pattern set event by event, when its insert listens. Its knobs but code::output::, unless the effect says which: leave out what reallocates, clicks or means nothing in steps.

METHOD:: renamed
Parameters a subclass renamed, as an Event old -> new. code::set::, code::get::, the properties, code::settings_:: and code::fullSettings:: given an old name reach the new one, so old presets and sessions still load.

METHOD:: currentKey
A parameter's name now, said by its old name or its new.
ARGUMENT:: key

METHOD:: usesTidalClock
Whether its graph reads FxLfo's cycle clock: SuperDirtGUI then tells it Tidal's cps and cycle. False unless the effect says so.

METHOD:: knob
A table entry. box says how the value reads: code::\ms::, code::\pct::, code::\note:: (a Tidal note name, 60 is c5) or nil. lag is the control's smoothing, 0.05 unless said. section is the card it goes in; nil has no widget. The last three are for the device panel's knob and are ignored by link::Classes/FxParamPanel::. Any other key can be put on the entry afterwards: code::menuItems:: (pairs of label and value for the knob's right click menu).
ARGUMENT:: key
ARGUMENT:: lo
ARGUMENT:: hi
ARGUMENT:: warp
ARGUMENT:: default
ARGUMENT:: units
ARGUMENT:: label
ARGUMENT:: hint
ARGUMENT:: box
ARGUMENT:: lag
ARGUMENT:: section
ARGUMENT:: dec
ARGUMENT:: step
ARGUMENT:: size
code::\big::, code::\medium:: (when nil) or code::\small::: the one to three that define the effect are big.
ARGUMENT:: bipolar
Whether the arc starts at the top: worked out as true for a range like -1 .. 1 or -24 .. 24; say false for one that only looks symmetric.
ARGUMENT:: floorText
What the bottom of the range reads when it means off, as code::"off"::.

METHOD:: menu
A table entry. In the device panel, a menu of two to four items is a row of lit segments; put code::style: \knob:: on the entry for a knob that steps through the items (note values), or code::style: \menu:: to keep a menu.
ARGUMENT:: key
ARGUMENT:: items
ARGUMENT:: default
ARGUMENT:: label
ARGUMENT:: hint
ARGUMENT:: section

METHOD:: toggle
A table entry. In the device panel it is one lit segment; put code::size: \big:: on the entry for a pad - a click latches it, holding it past a third of a second makes it momentary (freeze while held).
ARGUMENT:: key
ARGUMENT:: default
ARGUMENT:: label
ARGUMENT:: hint
ARGUMENT:: section

METHOD:: mixParams
The two table entries most effects end on: code::mix:: and code::output::.
ARGUMENT:: section
ARGUMENT:: mix

METHOD:: ar
The effect as a UGen, reading a control called name.
ARGUMENT:: in
ARGUMENT:: name
ARGUMENT:: bypass

METHOD:: table
The table, worked out once.

METHOD:: paramNames
Its keys, in order.

METHOD:: param
One entry.
ARGUMENT:: key

METHOD:: specs
The knobs' ControlSpecs, by key.

METHOD:: sections
The rows, in the order the table first says them.

METHOD:: presetNames
The menu: built in, then yours.

METHOD:: addPreset
Keep some settings under a name.
ARGUMENT:: name
ARGUMENT:: settings

METHOD:: removePreset
Drop one of yours.
ARGUMENT:: name

METHOD:: hasClipboard
Whether something has been copied, of this class.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: tidal
Something that plays it from Tidal - SuperDirtGUI's insert - or nil. It answers code::listening::, code::listening_::, code::tidalName:: and code::tidalKeys::, and says code::changed(\tidal):: on the unit when they change. With one, the window shows a listen switch and, while it listens, each parameter Tidal can set labelled in green by its key.

METHOD:: isListening
Whether link::#-tidal:: is listening now.

METHOD:: tidalKeys
The keys a pattern sets while it listens, else an empty Array.

METHOD:: set
Set a parameter by name. A knob clips to its range.
ARGUMENT:: key
ARGUMENT:: value

METHOD:: get
Read one by name.
ARGUMENT:: key

METHOD:: settings
All of them, as an Event: what a preset and the clipboard hold. Setting it ignores what it does not know and leaves alone what it does not say.

METHOD:: presetName
Which preset these settings are, or nil.

METHOD:: saveAsPreset
Keep them, in this session and the next.
ARGUMENT:: name

METHOD:: copySettings
Put them aside, for one of the same class.

METHOD:: pasteSettings
Take them.

METHOD:: copyFrom
Take another's.
ARGUMENT:: other

METHOD:: meter
Whether its node reports levels. A window turns this on while it is open.

METHOD:: inLevel
The input's peak, from the last reply.

METHOD:: outLevel
The output's.

METHOD:: extras
What the DSP asked to report, from the last reply.

METHOD:: isAlive
Whether a reply has come in the last second.

